.TH SET_FS 2
.SH NAME
set_fs \- this thread's thread pointer, for thread-local storage (SYS_SET_FS)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

syscall4(SYS_SET_FS, address, 0, 0, 0);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B SYS_SET_FS
sets the calling thread's thread pointer to
.IR address :
the FS base on x86-64, TPIDR_EL0 on arm64. Compilers reach thread-local
variables
.RB ( _Thread_local )
at a fixed offset from it. The kernel loads it on every switch to the
thread.
.PP
The library does it for each thread
.RB ( thread_start (2)):
it copies the program's template of thread-local variables and places it
by the processor's convention: on x86-64 just below the thread pointer,
whose own word holds its address (%fs:0); on arm64 16 bytes above it,
past a control block. Programs without thread-local variables skip this.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I address
is not a user address.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR thread_start (2),
.BR sys_thread_self (2)
